## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

**10 tests analyzed · 8 Critical · 1 Medium · 1 Low**

This suite is the textbook **coverage-touching** anti-pattern. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called — in near-declaration order — but almost nothing is verified. That's why you hit 95% coverage yet keep finding production bugs: the tests prove the code *runs*, not that it *works*.

---

### 🔴 CRITICAL — Tests giving false confidence

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (systemic — all 10 tests)

The entire class methodically walks through every method on `ProductCatalog` (Add → Get → Update → Remove → Search → GetAll → Count → BulkDiscount → Export → Import) with either **no assertion at all** or only a trivial `Assert.IsNotNull` that proves nothing about correctness.

#### 2. No Assertions (5 tests)

| Test (line) | What it calls | What it verifies |
|---|---|---|
| `AddProduct_Works` (L17) | `AddProduct` | ❌ Nothing |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` (L31) | `UpdatePrice` | ❌ Nothing |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` (L38) | `RemoveProduct` | ❌ Nothing |
| `GetProductCount_Works` (L63) | `GetProductCount` | ❌ Nothing (result discarded) |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L85) | `ImportFromCsv` | ❌ Nothing |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L68) | `ApplyBulkDiscount` | ❌ Nothing |

**Fix example — `AddProduct_Works`:**
```csharp
// BEFORE (L17-21) — passes even if AddProduct silently drops the product
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_Works()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
}

// AFTER — verifies the product was actually persisted
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

    var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.IsNotNull(stored);
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored.Price);
}
```

**Fix example — `UpdatePrice_Works`:**
```csharp
// BEFORE (L31-36) — passes even if UpdatePrice is a no-op
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_Works()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
}

// AFTER — verifies price actually changed
[TestMethod]
